  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\n- Showers/storms likely Tuesday night into Wednesday\n\nHigh pressure continues in control of the weather this afternoon \nwith seasonable temperatures for early September and much lower \nhumidity levels compared to last week. This drier weather will \ncontinue through most of the day Tuesday for most areas.\n\nVery late on Tuesday, the rain chances will begin to increase across \nthe NW corner of the area, with rain spreading SE through Wednesday \nmorning. This increase in rain chances will be via a 30+ knot low \nlevel jet advecting decent moisture ahead of the incoming cold \nfront. \n\nThe thunder threat looks to be a scattered to numerous one with \nfairly low chances of anything severe. The low level jet moving in \nwill provide enough elevated instability Tuesday night for thunder. \nIt is not very strong, only around a few hundred J/kg at best. This \nis due to the timing of the front approaching during the coolest \ntime of the day, and with a lack of support aloft from a cold \npool/short wave. \n\nShowers and storms will be most likely in the morning, then \ngradually moving out from NW to SE during the afternoon hours. This \nis a bit too early for any significant destabilization to occur \nduring peak heating.\n\nWe can't rule out an isolated shower on Thursday afternoon as a weak \nupper trough will be moving through. This trough could combine with \nsome lake breeze convergence to pop a shower or two. The vast \nmajority of the area and day will be dry. \n\n- Rain possible Saturday\n\nHigh pressure will be over the area Thursday night and Friday, \nbefore shifting east of the area Friday night. There is a short wave \nmoving into Ontario that will drag another cold front into the area \non Saturday. \n\nThis front looks to have much less rain potential with it for \nmultiple reasons. The first is that the upper support once again \nstays north of the area, limiting the upper level synoptic support. \nIn addition, the front does not have a good strong moist flow ahead \nof it from the Gulf. If anything, the trend in the data has been for \nless and less rain to be expected.\n\n- Stronger system possible Monday with rain and wind\n\nWe see a brief break on Sunday before the next system approaches on \nMonday. This system has the potential to be a much stronger system \nthan the two systems earlier in the period. \n\nWe will see a stronger upper low drop into the Pacific NW by next \nweekend. It will then rotate eastward across the northern tier of \nthe country late next weekend into early next week. It is expected \nto support a stronger sfc low over the Upper Midwest that will be \ndriven ENE, and approach the region. \n\nWe could see the initial rain with this system as early as late \nSunday night into Monday morning with a leading short wave, and/or \nthe warm air advection low level jet supporting the chances. Then, \nafter a period of potentially being in the warm sector, the cold \nfront will move in fairly strong. This system looks like right now \nthat it will have a favorable feed of rich Gulf moisture ahead of \nit. Of course details with regard to timing and exact track this far \nout are still uncertain. This system could cause some impacts with \nrain, storms, and wind.",
